WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.193
- Welcome back, y'all.

00:00:01.193 --> 00:00:02.640
I'm here with Garth Brooks,

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.670
and we're surrounded by an
audience of his biggest fans.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.250
Give it up for Garth, everybody.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.416
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:00:09.416 --> 00:00:10.673
Garth is an American icon,

00:00:10.673 --> 00:00:13.620
and not just for his crowd-stomping hits.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:16.230
His music has been a source
of comfort and healing

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.370
in moments of tragedy.

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.870
I just literally went
through his whole catalog,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.550
there's so many, in my head,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.072
but we've spoken about
your song "The Change."

00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:24.060
- Yes.
- I love "The Change" so much

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.460
and its connection to the
Oklahoma City bombing.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.560
How do you, I know that we're musicians,

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.700
so obviously music affects us,

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.960
but how does music help
you get through tragedy?

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.910
- Yeah, I mean it's,
music is a great healer,

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.260
but it's also that whole thing.

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:38.648
You ever watch "Ted Lasso?"

00:00:38.648 --> 00:00:39.630
- (laughs) Yes.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.677
- He's got this thing with
this kid when he gets mad.

00:00:41.677 --> 00:00:43.770
"Count to 10. If that
doesn't work, go again."

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.240
But I think that's what music does.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:48.150
It gives you that minute to
sit and think about it, right?

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:49.980
And I think that's what music does.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.100
Music brings you a peace that
gets all the clutter out,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.140
and hopefully you can make good decisions

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.130
for you and everyone around you.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.070
- It's such a beautiful, positive outlet.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:00:59.940
- Yeah, amen.
- And it's a nice way

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:00.930
to point out that we don't always

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.040
have to disagree on everything.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.200
So you took to social media

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:06.660
after the school shooting in Nashville

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.090
where three children and
three adults lost their lives,

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.770
and that was close to you, right?

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.603
- Yeah, it was tough.

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:13.470
We were, I was going in the studio,

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.020
and out on the highway,
you never, ever see it,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.360
but here comes all the ambulances.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.670
Here comes fire trucks,
everything on the shoulder

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:23.130
just blowing by, and it
was like more than one.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.930
And it just came, and
then no matter what street

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.430
you turned off, they were
passing you that way, too.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:29.096
And you're thinking, you just-

00:01:29.096 --> 00:01:29.929
- Something has happened.
- I don't know

00:01:29.929 --> 00:01:32.010
if I've gotten so used to it,
but I didn't think anything

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.690
until I got to the studio,
where it was happening.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.810
Studio was about a mile and a
half from where it happened.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.250
And yeah, this was a very sobering day

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:42.720
for all of us around the planet, I'm sure.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.450
But something about being
that close brings it a little-

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.700
- That's the thing,
right, the direct impact.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.060
I think we, it's unfortunate,
but we're almost desensitized.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:52.140
You hear it, I was saying in the break,

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.110
like it's like there's
so many school shootings.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.690
It's like there's so,
this is happening so often

00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:00.570
that I think, you know, when
you have that direct impact

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.370
in your community and it happens,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.770
there's no way you can
translate that to someone,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:07.170
that effect, like what
it does to not only you

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:09.660
but everyone around you,
those families, everyone,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.430
the domino effect of that.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.680
But since the tragedy
at the Covenant School,

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:15.420
the Tennessee state capitol has seen

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:18.960
thousands of protestors, mostly
students, demanding action.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.450
Two of the organizers are dialed in today.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.490
They're both students
at Vanderbilt University

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.590
and members of March For Our Lives.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.110
Everybody say hello to Brynn and Ezri.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.540
Hi, ladies.
(audience applauding)

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:32.550
So Brynn and Ezri, meet Garth.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:34.041
Say hey.
- How you doing?

00:02:34.041 --> 00:02:35.100
- [Brynn and Ezri] Hi!

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.417
- [Garth] Very nice to meet you.

00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:39.120
- You must have that
effect, giggly, bubbly hi.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:39.953
- [Garth] It's very sweet.

00:02:39.953 --> 00:02:40.786
- That was awesome.

00:02:40.786 --> 00:02:44.010
So both of you are very
young, but sadly the fight is,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.930
this fight isn't new to
either of you, right?

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.030
You've grown up with this, right?

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.250
- Yeah, I mean, we definitely
have grown up with this.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.380
I remember when I was in fifth grade,

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.000
I came home from school one day,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.040
and my mom like hugged me extra tight,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.890
and it was the day that
Sandy Hook happened.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.990
And I kind of remember from then on out

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:02.940
thinking about like these exit strategies

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:03.837
when I was in classrooms, you know,

00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:06.450
and it was something I
noticed my classmates

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:07.283
thinking about more.

00:03:07.283 --> 00:03:09.180
You know, where would we hide?

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.220
Could we all fit behind the desk?

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.940
Could we like put desks in
front of the door, you know?

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.820
Did the teacher have a baseball
bat that was sitting there?

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.320
I remember thinking about my brother, too,

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.910
and his elementary school next door

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.280
and what I would do if
there was something there.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.340
- Yeah, the lockdown
generation is very much

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.410
what we've grown up in, and
we know that gun violence

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.750
is the leading cause of
death for young people,

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.080
which means that none of us
really escape from this impact.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.970
And almost every single one of our peers

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.340
has a story just like
Brynn's, I know I do.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.200
And for me, I've actually been involved

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.520
in March For Our Lives for five years

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.130
when it started out in response
to the Parkland shooting

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.380
where I organized a
walkout at my middle school

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.820
where I was only 13 years old.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.310
And so I've very much
grown up in this movement

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.770
and along with hundreds of other kids

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.410
as young as elementary
school-aged children

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:57.210
all the way up into university age,

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.060
which is exactly who
we're organizing with now.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.690
But it's absolutely tragic that
we feel responsible for this

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.820
because the adults aren't listening

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:06.930
- 100%, they are not.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.150
And then also, it's just insane to me,

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:10.470
like, I'm a mom, you're a father,

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.530
like, my kids go through drills at school

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.910
at such a young age already.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.140
They have to be prepared for that.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.030
And like, I remember when we were kids,

00:04:18.030 --> 00:04:19.890
the biggest thing we did
at Texas and Oklahoma

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:20.908
were like tornado drills.

00:04:20.908 --> 00:04:21.741
- Tornado drills, fire drills.

00:04:21.741 --> 00:04:22.574
- Yeah, that's always, it was like,

00:04:22.574 --> 00:04:24.180
"Oh, just be in the hallway
and put your hands,"

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:28.080
and you know, it was just like,
it's such a different world.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.270
Like, do you remember
when your kids came home

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.010
like having to talk about shooter drills?

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:33.000
Did they have to do anything like that?

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.440
- Sure, especially with social media,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.060
the world becomes smaller.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:38.070
So what you guys go through now,

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.470
we would've never known
before social media, right?

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:42.240
But now everybody knows.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.660
All I can say is shame on my generation.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:48.930
If we're the cause, why that's
what you have to think about

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:49.763
while you're in school,

00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:52.200
because kids should be thinking about

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.970
what they're going to do
with the rest of their life

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:57.090
and just have fun with their
mates and just grow up, so.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.050
- It's already hard enough, you know?

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.960
- Amen, it sure is, so yeah,

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:02.550
what I love about you
guys, and what I'm gonna

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:07.550
encourage you to do is keep
the conversation active.

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:11.310
At some point we're all gonna
get to the right answer.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.710
It's just, and we're gonna need
all of us to get there, too.

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.977
- Both helped organize the massive protest

00:05:15.977 --> 00:05:18.510
at the Tennessee state
capitol this spring.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:20.280
So who's joining you there?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.260
- Yeah, I mean, I think that we see

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:23.700
almost everyone joining us.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.320
One of the things that I've loved the most

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:28.470
is seeing how many different
students are coming out.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:30.870
We're both students at
Vanderbilt University,

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.010
but we had students

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:34.710
from every single
Nashville college join us.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:37.620
We had elementary schoolers,
middle and high schoolers.

00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:39.210
We had people all the way down

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:40.890
from Franklin and Rutherford County,

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:42.930
and we also had people
that were up the street.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.730
And I think that what
that demonstrates to me

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.620
is for kids in our generation,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:48.720
this isn't a Republican-Democrat issue.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.300
It's not a red or blue
issue. It's just an issue.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.710
It's just something that we all care about

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:53.543
because it's something

00:05:53.543 --> 00:05:55.260
that we are constantly thinking about.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.540
- Yeah, and so along with
all of those students

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.670
in coalition with us,
I also wanna emphasize

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:01.830
that while this started
as a Nashville walkout

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:04.320
that we had the honor of
being able to organize,

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.170
there's been continuous action
at the capitol every day

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.510
since this mass shooting has happened.

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:11.610
And what I've had the
privilege of meeting people

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.110
who are as young as six years old

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.180
all the way to people
who are 80 years old.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.680
We have people coming in in buses

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.500
from Memphis and Knoxville
coming down to support this.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.420
This is such a broad coalition,

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.820
and it's bigger than
just a student movement

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:25.800
even though students are the
ones who are leading this.

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.840
We have a huge support from the community,

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:29.340
which is absolutely imperative,

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:32.040
and we also have huge
support from organizations

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:33.960
that are building coalition in a way

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.180
that the state hasn't seen in decades

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:37.980
because this is an issue that impacts

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:39.420
every single person in the community.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.500
I mean, like you were talking about,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.540
yes, students are fearful for their lives

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.560
and we're losing our peers,

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.600
but parents and families are fearful

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.580
for their students and
losing their children.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.130
And we also have this resounding
impact in the community

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:53.100
that this gun violence touches
every single one of us.

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.650
And what this past couple
of weeks have shown us

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:58.140
is that every single one of us is fed up,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.400
and every single one of us is ready

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.140
to stand together in action.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.583
- Totally not gonna cry.

00:07:03.431 --> 00:07:04.830
- I don't know how you can't.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:06.840
You're right in the middle
of it with your babies.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:11.840
- Yeah, like, it's just such
a, it's such an obvious thing.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.340
It's such a logical fix.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.200
Like, you know, and he is right.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:18.420
You keep speaking, everybody
keeps talking about it.

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.130
This is how real change happens.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.050
So thank you so much for doing that.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:24.270
So Nashville is the country music capital,

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.040
and musicians are playing their part.

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:26.873
And how are they doing it?

00:07:26.873 --> 00:07:28.800
They do benefits, is
this what's happening?

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.480
- Yeah, actually bigger than benefits.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.610
We've had musicians at every single rally,

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:35.700
at every single protest
joining us, standing behind us.

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:37.470
We've had Nashville Musicians for Change,

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:39.720
which is an absolutely
incredible organization,

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.550
who's come out, and they've really honored

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:42.960
like the tradition of Nashville.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:44.670
Like you said, it is Music City.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.220
And so they've come out and
they've encouraged the crowd.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.080
And like you were talking about earlier,

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.270
we acknowledge that music
is a very healing space.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:53.400
And so there's a lot of
pain in Nashville right now.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.410
There's a lot of pain in Tennessee,

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.510
but this beautiful moment of community

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.000
that's come out of it is
really exemplified in music.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.550
And so there's been a lot of
honoring of the folk tradition

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.490
of having the crowd sing and
join in with the singers.

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:07.830
We've had a lot of country
artists come out and join us.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.350
I mean, we had Emmylou
Harris and Margo Price

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.810
and Sam Bush and all of these artists

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:14.550
who are turning out in force for us

00:08:14.550 --> 00:08:16.470
and showing up every single day

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:17.880
and taking time out of their days

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.770
to make sure that they're
engaging and connecting

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.660
with the crowd in a really powerful way,

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:23.490
and it's made a noticeable difference.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.110
We've also had gospel singers.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:27.750
I mean, the tradition of music
in the South and in Nashville

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:30.480
is so deep and rich,
and it's very apparent

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:32.040
at these protests because these protests

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:33.750
is the whole community.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:34.583
- Absolutely.

00:08:34.583 --> 00:08:35.550
- Sweet, very smart.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:40.350
You girls at this young age,
I'm four times your age.

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:43.350
I'm telling you right now,
I don't have the courage.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.170
I don't have the whatever it takes

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.480
from the fear of everything.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.330
What you're doing, I can't
tell you is great enough.

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:52.920
And I don't feel like you're wanting to,

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:54.750
you're trying to change the world.

00:08:54.750 --> 00:08:56.370
You're asking the people
that are in control

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.830
to please just take a look at it.

00:08:58.830 --> 00:08:59.663
- Yeah.

00:08:59.663 --> 00:09:01.350
- We're all victims.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:04.740
We're all future victims from it.

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:08.550
I will tell you this
though, in our industry,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.160
the greatest thing that you receive,

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:12.930
that you look to kind
of show they appreciate

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.090
what the music has done for your life,

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.010
in our world is a standing ovation.

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.452
May I be the first to start
it for you two girls today?

00:09:19.452 --> 00:09:23.216
- Oh, yes!
(all applauding)

00:09:23.216 --> 00:09:26.233
(audience cheering)

00:09:26.233 --> 00:09:29.973
- Keep fighting, keep talking,
keep the discussion alive.

00:09:31.814 --> 00:09:36.477
- Oh, my gosh, our show
supports Brynn and Ezri

00:09:36.477 --> 00:09:39.330
and the work that they're
doing to end gun violence.

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:42.240
We truly believe bringing
more voices together

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:43.770
can help solve this crisis.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.470
I mean, obviously, and I
love that music is helping

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.490
in how you're saying as well.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.500
And I know that whether
